Based on [1], the `__iter__` method (part of `SyncProjection`, described in [2]) works as a pull-based, replay-capable iterator over buffered deltas:

- It maintains a local `cursor` starting at 0, so each call to `__iter__` creates a fresh cursor and replays all deltas from the start [2].
- Before iterating, it calls `self._ensure_started()` (if set) to lazily start the underlying stream [1].
- In a loop:
  - If `cursor < len(self._deltas)`, it yields `self._deltas[cursor]` and increments `cursor` — this is the normal "catch up on buffered data" path [1].
  - If the cursor has caught up to the buffer (`cursor >= len(self._deltas)`):
    - If `self._error` is set, it raises that error.
    - Else if `self._done` is `True`, it returns (stream finished, nothing more to yield).
    - Else if a `self._request_more` callback is installed, it enters an inner loop that repeatedly calls `self._request_more()` to pull more events from the producer, continuing until either the cursor is no longer caught up (`cursor < len(self._deltas)`) or `self._done` becomes true. If `_request_more()` returns falsy, the inner loop breaks.
      - After the inner loop, if the cursor is still caught up (no new deltas arrived), it checks for an error and raises it, or otherwise returns.
    - If no `_request_more` callback exists, it simply returns.

